\(90 \\times 90\\)).\n   - Multiply 91 by 3 (which is \\(91 \\times 3\\)).\n   - Multiply 1 by 90 (which is \\(1 \\times 90\\)).\n   - Multiply 1 by 3 (which is \\(1 \\times 3\\)).\n\n3. **Calculate each partial product**:\n   - \\(91 \\times 90 = 8190\\)\n   - \\(91 \\times 3 = 273\\)\n   - \\(1 \\times 90 = 90\\)\n   - \\(1 \\times 3 = 3\\)\n\n4. **Add the partial products**:\n   - Add the results of the partial products together.\n\n```\n  8190\n+ 273\n+ 90\n+ 3\n-----\n 8463\n```\n\nSo, the product of 91 and 93 is 8463.\n\nThis method breaks down the multiplication into smaller, more manageable parts and helps in visualizing the process step by step.\n</think>.\n<answer>. Therefore, the product of 91 and 93, solved using the partial products method, is 8463.</answer>.<|User|>Could you also provide an example of how to solve this problem using the area model method for better visualization?\nPlease reason step by step.<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c><think>.\nCertainly! The area model method is a visual way to break down multiplication into smaller, more manageable parts. To solve \\(91 \\times 93\\) using the area model method, we can think of it as breaking down the numbers into their place values and then finding the area of a rectangle.\n\n1. **Break down the numbers**:\n   - \\(91\\) can be broken down into \\(90 + 1\\).\n   - \\(93\\) can be broken down into \\(90 + 3\\).\n\n2. **Set up the area model**:\n   - Draw a rectangle and divide it into four smaller rectangles.\n   - Label the sides of the large rectangle with the decomposed numbers: \\(90\\) and \\(1\\) on one side, and \\(90\\) and \\(3\\) on the other side.\n\n```\n  90 |  1\n    |____|\n  90 |  3\n    |____|\n```\n\n3. **Calculate the area of each smaller rectangle**:\n   - The area of the top-left rectangle is \\(90 \\times 90 = 8100\\).\n   - The area of the top-right rectangle is \\(90 \\times 1 = 90\\).\n   - The area of the bottom-left rectangle is \\(1 \\times 90 = 90\\).\n   - The area of the bottom-right rectangle is \\(1 \\times 3 = 3\\).\n\n4. **Add the areas of the smaller rectangles**:\n   - Sum the areas: \\(8100 + 90 + 90 + 3 = 8463\\).\n\nSo, the product of \\(91\\) and \\(93\\) is \\(8463\\).\n\nThis method visually represents the distributive property of multiplication and helps in understanding how each part of the numbers contributes to the final product.\n</think>.\n<answer>. Therefore, the product of 91 and 93, solved using the area model method, is 8463.</answer>.<|User|>Could you also provide an example of how to solve this problem using the standard algorithm for better understanding?\nPlease reason step by step.<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c><think>.\nCertainly! The standard algorithm is the most common and straightforward method for multiplying two numbers. Here's how to solve \\(91 \\times 93\\) using the standard algorithm:\n\n1. **Write the numbers in a vertical format**:\n   - Write 91 on top and 93 below it, aligning the digits by place value.\n\n```\n  91\n\u00d7 93\n```\n\n2. **Multiply 91 by the ones digit of 93 (which is 3)**:\n   - Multiply 91 by 3.\n   - \\( 91 \\times 3 = 273 \\).\n\n   Write down 273 below the line, aligning the digits by place value.\n\n```\n  91\n\u00d7 93\n-----\n 273\n```\n\n3. **Multiply 91 by the tens digit of 93 (which is 9), and remember to shift the result one place to the left**:\n   - Multiply 91 by 9, and remember to shift the result one place to the left (because it's in the tens place).\n   - \\( 91 \\times 9 = 819 \\).\n\n   Write down 819 below the previous result, aligning the digits by place value.\n\n```\n  91\n\u00d7 93\n-----\n 273\n8190\n```\n\n4. **Add the two results together**:\n   - Add the numbers in the first row (273) to the numbers in the second row (8190).\n\n```\n  91\n\u00d7 93\n-----\n 273\n8190\n-----\n 8463\n```\n\nSo, the product of 91 and 93 is 8463.\n\nThis method involves breaking down the multiplication into smaller steps and then summing the results, making it a systematic an