      "text": "9991.</answer>.<|User|>The method you used is quite interesting. Can you explain why the difference of squares formula works in this case?</\uff5cAssistant\uff5c><think>.\nCertainly! The difference of squares formula, \\((a - b)(a + b) = a^2 - b^2\\), works because it takes advantage of the algebraic identity that simplifies the multiplication of two binomials. Let's break it down step by step:\n\n1. **Identify the structure**: We have two numbers, 97 and 91, which are close to each other. We can express them in terms of a common base number, 100, and a difference or sum of numbers.\n\n2. **Rewrite the numbers**: \n   \\[ 97 = 100 - 3 \\]\n   \\[ 91 = 100 - 9 \\]\n\n3. **Apply the difference of squares formula**: \n   \\[ (100 - 3)(100 - 9) = 100^2 - 3^2 \\]\n\n4. **Calculate the squares**:\n   \\[ 100^2 = 10000 \\]\n   \\[ 3^2 = 9 \\]\n\n5. **Subtract the squares**:\n   \\[ 10000 - 9 = 9991 \\]\n\nThe formula works because it leverages the algebraic identity that simplifies the multiplication of binomials. When you expand \\((a - b)(a + b)\\), you get:\n\\[ (a - b)(a + b) = a^2 + ab - ab - b^2 = a^2 - b^2 \\]\n\nIn our case, \\(a = 100\\) and \\(b = 3\\), so:\n\\[ (100 - 3)(100 + 3) = 100^2 - 3^2 = 10000 - 9 = 9991 \\]\n\nThis method is efficient because it reduces the complexity of the multiplication by breaking it down into simpler calculations involving squares and subtraction.\n</think>.\n<answer>Therefore, the difference of squares formula works because it simplifies the multiplication of two binomials by using the algebraic identity \\((a - b)(a + b) = a^2 - b^2\\). This identity allows us to transform the original multiplication problem into a subtraction problem involving squares, which is often easier to compute.</answer>",
