-+\n   ```\n4. Calculate the area of each smaller rectangle:\n   - The area of the top-left rectangle is 10 \u00d7 40 = 400.\n   - The area of the top-right rectangle is 10 \u00d7 9 = 90.\n   - The area of the bottom-left rectangle is 3 \u00d7 40 = 120.\n   - The area of the bottom-right rectangle is 3 \u00d7 9 = 27.\n5. Add the areas of the four smaller rectangles together:\n   ```\n   400\n   +90\n   +120\n   +27\n   ---\n   637\n   ```\nSo, the product of 13 and 49, solved using the area model method, is 637.\n</think>.\n<answer>. Therefore, the product of 13 and 49, solved using the area model method, is 637.</answer>.<|User|>Thank you for the detailed explanation using the area model method. Can you also show me how to solve this using the distributive property method?\nPlease reason step by step.<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c><think>.\nCertainly! The distributive property method is a way to break down multiplication by distributing the multiplication over addition. To solve the problem of finding the product of 13 and 49 using the distributive property method, we can follow these steps:\n1. Break down 49 into 50 - 1, as we did earlier, to make the multiplication easier.\n2. Rewrite the multiplication as: 13 \u00d7 (50 - 1).\n3. Use the distributive property of multiplication over subtraction to split this into two separate multiplications: (13 \u00d7 50) - (13 \u00d7 1).\n4. Calculate each part separately:\n   - 13 \u00d7 50 = 650.\n   - 13 \u00d7 1 = 13.\n5. Subtract the second result from the first: 650 - 13 = 637.\nSo, the product of 13 and 49, solved using the distributive property method, is 637.\n</think>.\n<answer>. Therefore, the product of 13 and 49, solved using the distributive property method, is 637.</answer>.<|User|>Thank you for the detailed explanation using the distributive property method. Can you also show me how to solve this using the repeated addition method?\nPlease reason step by step.<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c><think>.\nCertainly! The repeated add