6 * 50):\n   ```\n   16\n   \u00d7\n   50\n   \u2014\u2014\n   800\n   ```\n3. Calculate the area of the second smaller rectangle (16 * -1):\n   ```\n   16\n   \u00d7\n   -1\n   \u2014\u2014\n   -16\n   ```\n4. Add the areas of the smaller rectangles together:\n   ```\n   800\n   +(-16)\n   \u2014\u2014\n   784\n   ```\nTherefore, using the area model method, the product of 16 and 49 is 784.\n</think>.\n<answer>. Therefore, using the area model method, the product of 16 and 49 is 784.</answer>.<|User|>Thank you for the detailed explanation using the area model method. Can you also provide an example of how to solve this problem using the lattice method?\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c><think>.\nCertainly! The lattice method is a visual and systematic way to multiply two numbers. To solve the problem of finding the product of 16 and 49 using the lattice method, we can follow these steps:\n1. Draw a 2x2 grid (since we are multiplying a 2-digit number by a 2-digit number).\n2. Write the digits of 16 along the top of the grid and the digits of 49 along the right side of the grid.\n3. Divide each cell of the grid into two triangles, one for the tens place and one for the ones place.\n4. Multiply the digits that correspond to each cell of the grid, placing the tens digit in the top triangle and the ones digit in the right triangle.\n5. Add the numbers in each diagonal, carrying over if necessary.\nLet's go through the steps:\n1. Draw a 2x2 grid and write the digits as follows:\n   ```\n   16\n   \u00d7\n   49\n   \u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\n   16\n   \u00d7\n   49\n   \u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\n   ```\n2. Multiply the digits that correspond to each cell of the grid:\n   ```\n   16\n   \u00d7\n   49\n   \u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\n   16\n   \u00d7\n   49\n   \u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\n   16\n   \u00d7\n   49\n   \u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\n   16\n   \u00d7\n   49\n   \u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\n   ```\n   ```\n   16\n   \u00d7\n   49\n   \u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\n   16\n   \u00d7\n   49\n  